    {
      "paperId": "d46da9fe57605e0005b75f77c89e4f80a17e2ee1",
      "title": "Analisando a colonialidade do poder como caracteristica da criminologia moderna no sistema-mundo europeu / Analyzing the coloniality of power as a characteristic of modern criminology in the European world-system",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Lorenna Verally Rodrigues dos Santos",
          "authorId": "98881136"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2022,
      "abstract": "This research seeks to reflect on the theme of the coloniality of power and its ramifications through the analysis of social sciences in Latin America. Knowing that the coloniality of power has a fundamental task regarding the structure of the \"world-system\", and that modern criminology had as its main idealizer Cesare Lombroso, who took advantage of scientificity to direct a profile of the criminal based on Western European stereotypes. The following question was raised: How does the coloniality of power characterize modern criminology in the European world-system? Our general objective was, to observe the reflexes of the decolonial turn process in the new Latin American constitutionalism. And specific objectives were Analyze how postcolonialism affects the coloniality of power; verify the consequences of the coloniality of power and eurocentrism in criminology. To think about the decolonial turn as a movement towards a new Latin American constitutionalism. The main authors approached are names like: Mignolo, Quijano, Barbosa and Teixeira, Schwarcz, and Machado. We had as methodological aspects, the qualitative approach, bibliographical research, on the research procedure we used the descriptive, with exploratory research, and data analysis through the technique of content analysis. Conclusion: The difficult paths taken by all of humanity that did not fit into the \"suit and tie\" standards, i.e., the Eurocentric standard, are perceived throughout. We saw that the consequences of the Lombrosian theory, stigmatizing the \"criminal profile\", where he based himself on science to express purely political desires. In this sense, decolonizing the teroria, is above all decolonizing Eurocentric power.",
